{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1491435-fae-immortality-and-reincarnation-through-their-mortal-hosts","id":1491435,"title":"fae immortality and reincarnation through their mortal hosts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-08-26T12:27:02-07:00","id":1491435,"title":"fae immortality and reincarnation through their mortal hosts","content":"How do you handle the immortality of the Fae essence and its ongoing reincarnation in mortal flesh? While this is mentioned in the material, I have not seen this addressed to any degree.<br /> <br /> I would think the Changeling would not remember their previous mortal selves. I think the Mists would take care of that, since the mortal flesh they once wore would now be immaterial. But the game implies the Changeling would remember their lives throughout the ages. That means their lives before the Shattering as well as the time periods after their various Chrysalis until their mortal host died.<br /> <br /> Do you have their full memory return immediately or soon after the Chrysalis? This would mean even Childer remember themselves in earlier time periods when they were not children. Or do their memories only slowly come back? Perhaps when reminded by older Changelings who bring up old stories. Or sometimes not all all? Does their memory depend on their permanent Banality so that after a point, the Changeling begins to forget as they become closer to being Undone?<br /> <br /> Although I do not play Changeling myself (I study the setting in order to use it in other game lines when needed), when speaking to friends who do play, this seems something often neglected. They concentrate on what has happened in the lifetime of the mortal seeming, not the extended backstory of their fae self which could go back hundreds or thousands of years.<br /> <br /> I know this is partially addressed by the Remembrance background, but that seems mainly about adding dice to an Etiquette pool or allowing the ST to help out the PC act appropriately. I see a lot more issues as a result. Since low levels imply no or little memory and high levels very good memories, do STs allow this background to increase over time as PCs learn more, encounter old friends or rivals, meet long living chimerical creatures, etc.? Or does it remain static and not given to either natural improvement (or increased with XP)?<br /> <br /> How should other Changelings react if a former owner of a Freehold or Treasure returns several decades later from their last incarnation? Does it legally revert to them, with the previous owners considered to be simply stewards?<br /> <br /> What are other implications of this in the setting?<br /> <br /> Curious as to how people handle this in their own games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-26T20:18:43-07:00","id":1491450,"title":"","content":"That's a complicated question, among other things because the setting brings to it our old friend that accompanies us throughout WoD: <b>inconsistency</b>.<br /> <br /> Different authors have put different twists in their stories, pieces of lore and characters, to the point that some argue that from a life to the next one maybe even a Changelings Kith may change, while for others that would be preposterous.<br /> <br /> My take is that memories from previous lives are fuzzy. How much varies a lot, as Changeling in particular is more about stories than anything. Not just because that's fun, but literally the Dreaming can affect those memories for its own reasons and the Changeling is more likely to remember (or give attention to) things that are dramatically relevant in this life.<br /> <br /> Probably memories are fuzzier the further in the past you go, but even if you remember everything most Changelings wouldn't pay much attention to past lives <b>or</b> would proceed with them as if they had just been sleeping. As many things in Changeling, this depends a lot on the person.<br /> <br /> For whatever it's worth, C20 explicitly embraces the idea that the Mists completely wipe out memory unless you have taken Remembrance, and raising it with time (and maybe XP) wouldn't be a bad choice, but I prefer a more open approach.<br /> <br /> Now, Oaths and supernatural rights and obligations are another matter entirely, not linked to how much you remember or not. There are Oaths specifically meant to last over lifetimes, as are curses and blessings. This does includes fealty and the Oath of the Freeholder.<br /> <br /> Commoners are already used to a thousand years living among mortals and focusing on their current life, so they're the more likely to let go old stuff for the sake of new <i>status quo</i>, although that can vary in a case-by-case basis. But nobles, that are either Arcadian Sidhe or have sworn fealty to them, usually assume some continuation, <i>specially</i> for their claims to the Freeholds. The Dreaming does seems to give credence to this, usually reinforcing the original Oaths over the new ones (the Oath of the Freeholder give many benefits inside the Freehold, so this is relevant).<br /> <br /> In the end, there are no hard and fast rules about the memories and continuation from one life to the other because this is one of the questions the characters are supposed to reflect upon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-26T21:12:57-07:00","id":1491453,"title":"","content":"Even though we use the term reincarnate, for the fae it actually isn't reincarnation as someone else. The fae identity should be consistent even though the mortal hosts may be radically different. Rumpelstiltskin the Nocker is still Rumpelstiltskin throughout the ages even though his mortal hosts were once Bob the Cook, then Tom the Cobbler, Fred the Constable, and so on. So the idea that Rumpelstiltskin doesn't care about his own past doesn't hold water to me. It should be like anyone else's personal history, even if Rumpelstiltskin has a lot of amnesia (and the ongoing memory issues associated with immortal beings living for a long time). I don't mind fae being mecurial creatures that aren't too beholden to the past, but always living in the now. But that should be because of their nature, not that they actually weren't the same fae. I think some form of continuity of its personality should persist. I view the existence inbetween mortal hosts to be something like &quot;sleep&quot;, not termination of the fae itself.<br /> <br /> But I do agree that previous mortal identities should be irrelevant.<br /> <br /> Now I understand you are saying that some authors of Changeling material don't go down that route. There's material that downplays the natural immortality/longevity of the fae. And that's fine. I'll just ignore that. It's not a route I'd choose to take, but I find Changeling the most flawed of all the WoD settings. It just doesn't seem very fairy-like to me even though I think a lot of the game's concepts are good. It's just the implementation that is bad.. So I try to use the concepts in a way that makes it easier to emulate actual fairy lore. Since you say the game is inconsistent, then it's quite open to individual ST interpretation.<br /> <br /> I do like the idea that Oaths, curses, and blessings do carry over from one incarnation to another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-27T07:26:04-07:00","id":1491471,"title":"","content":"In my games and head canon, on reawakening the Fae &quot;self&quot; of a Changeling is who they really are. They know that this is who they are, and have always been, but are fuzzy about the details. Like waking from a coma or deep dream.<br /> <br /> Clarity of the details of their fae lives is dependent on their Glamour vs Banality (treat as leaving the Dreaming in the &quot;Mists of Forgetfulness&quot; chart on page 304 of CtD 20th). Remembrance can be used similar to Werewolf's Past Lives background.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u201cHumpty had always sat on walls, it was his way.\u201d </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u2015 </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Lato\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Jasper Fforde, </span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/work/quotes/2504943\" target=\"_blank\">The Big Over Easy</a></span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":1756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","user":"MythAdvocate","date":". 08-27-2022, 11:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","name":"MythAdvocate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-27T10:15:03-07:00","id":1491490,"title":"","content":"Within Changeling the question of the Fae identity is a little tricky, although I do agree that there should be a strong sense of continuity. After all, they do culturally take mortal death mostly as a temporary setback, at least to a degree.<br /> <br /> Yet, the Changelings of CtD aren't about fairies of traditional folklore, they're about an amalgam creature unique to the setting more than any other splat. That's not a bug, it is how the game was intended to be, for better or worse. And the struggle to balance fae and mortal self is a key point to that.<br /> <br /> As a rule there is an intention to keep their identities through their several lives, and despite inconsistencies that does seem to be mostly the intended standard. Your initial question was about memory, though, so this is not exactly the same. Basically the fairy's identity is the same, and there's even a specific custom linked to this, the ritual of the Saining, when the True Name is revealed to the Changeling by a master of <b>Naming</b> and with it also their common fae name from past lives.<br /> <br /> But then, there are too many caveats that are what vary between characters. Forst caveat: memory is a mess. Arcadian Sidhe are only having to deal with this recently, but Autumn Changelings (the vast majority) started to draw some lines on the matter precisely because they had to adapt to memory loss between lives. What's the point of expecting total continuation from someone who don't remember the last time they were with you at all? Again, it is not that there's no expectation, but that they're used to deal with it in a case-by-case basis instead of set things in stone.<br /> <br /> The circumstances of the Autumn World also added a sense of change through time that the fae didn't had before. However Seelie they are, those that stayed learned that the world changes, politics change, and sometimes people change opinions, allegiances and needs. So while complete overhauls are rare, with a new life bringing also new circumstances, friends and foes, past relations are informative, but not completely reliable. If someone wants to use this new life to start over as a blank slate, why make a fuss out of it?<br /> <br /> S\u00edoch\u00e1in add an interesting observation to this, regardless of their actual rules or even existence. The point with them is to be immortal in a single incarnation, immortality of the human body the Changeling inhabits. That does show that while they know of their fairy soul's continuation, Changelings do actually value their current lives. They want it to last, it <b>is</b> important, even if flawed. Even the certainty of coming back in a new human life doesn't change that most Changelings don't want to physically die.<br /> <br /> Finally, and in conjunction with the previous observation, they are not <b>only</b> the fae soul. At least the vast majority aren't. When they undergo the Changeling Way, the result isn't a possession, that's a key difference. The soul of the average Changeling is an amalgam of sorts: it is both Fae and Human, and regardless of the Chrysalis, it is so from birth to death. Even the Undone are like that, as much as the fae aspect of the soul is just temporarily gone, the person is the same and no reincarnation will occur while that human lives. That's the point of the struggle between Banality and Wonder, Changelings are still as human as anyone else even if fairies <i><b>in addition</b></i> to that, and while past human lives may not matter, <b>the current one does</b>. A Changeling that leaves their previous mortal identity behind once they undergo Chrysalis isn't too different from anyone doing the same under any other splat or none at all. Changeling society has a place for them all the same, but this is not expected and not the most common outcome (if not particularly rare, either).<br /> <br /> Even the Arcadian Sidhe usually deal with that in some way. While they <b>are</b> possessors that throw away the original soul of that body, they usually enter babies (it's easier and, from their PoV, more ethical) and shut down their memories until Chrysalis. Whatever happens to that person while growing up, it was the fae's soul all along. They had parents, siblings, school, friends, sometimes even jobs before finding out what they are and recovering their memories. As much as the fae identity is old and continues, the mortal one isn't fake. It is just as real and relevant. And yeah, many still abandon those identities to become full time fairies, vut those are particularly prone to the threat of Bedlam. The only ones that don't go through that in any way are those who possess adults and need no Chrysalis. Even then, they still suffer some memory loss (that's important to keep the mysteries of Arcadia) and must learn to balance their new mundane existence as a part of their lives (being the <b>most</b> likely to fall to Bedlam).<br /> <br /> Now, the true point is that those things are at the core of the game's intended struggles. How to balance old ties to new circumstances? How do you deal with promises and duties you feel, but can't remember? How do you deal with those you remember, but the other party doesn't? What if old duties clash with new ones? What happens when old souls come to a new world? The setting has the Accordance War happening precisely because of those questions. Nobles come back from Arcadia with full expectations of continuity of the old ways and relations, but find a world where Commoners adapted to put this sense of continuity in check, even if not in any way completely ignored. They are forced to deal with the reasons the Commoners changed without previous experience to prepare them, but they also have at their side old magic that still agrees with their notions. It is a mess, but it is a mess by design.<br /> <br /> Just a final curiosity: nothing in the setting specifically says that new fairy souls can't ever be born, and hints to this have been given here and there, although again, inconsistent, and with little thought paid to them. So it may very well be that not all Changelings that undergo Chrysalis are really old souls. Fairies had babies in the past and possibly still have sometimes. The scattered hints point that the child would still be a Changeling (at least if born of two Changeling parents in the Autumn World), and there's even a plot point on High-King David expecting to soon have a baby as a means to avoid a new inheritance crisis for the crown. My own take is that most of the time such a child would be Kinnain rather than a true Fae, but the possibility still exists and isn't unheard of.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-27T14:17:05-07:00","id":1491519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MythAdvocate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1491435-fae-immortality-and-reincarnation-through-their-mortal-hosts?p=1491471#post1491471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my games and head canon, on reawakening the Fae &quot;self&quot; of a Changeling is who they really are. They know that this is who they are, and have always been, but are fuzzy about the details. Like waking from a coma or deep dream.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is along the lines I am thinking for my own chronicles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MythAdvocate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1491435-fae-immortality-and-reincarnation-through-their-mortal-hosts?p=1491471#post1491471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Clarity of the details of their fae lives is dependent on their Glamour vs Banality (treat as leaving the Dreaming in the &quot;Mists of Forgetfulness&quot; chart on page 304 of CtD 20th). Remembrance can be used similar to Werewolf's Past Lives background.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thinking of something similar to Glamour vs Banality, but couldn't figure out how to easily adjudicate that. I don't have C20 and can't see that chart. I do have 2e corebook and that has a Mists chart, but it only tracks Banality.<br /> <br /> I did consider Remembrance as something like Ancestors, but the game mechanic benefit doesn't have much to do with the questions of fae identity. However, if players wanted Remembrance to be more useful than it is in the corebook, I'd have no problem adding that as a benefit. But as I said, I actually don't play or run Changeling. I'm just working through some concepts to better understand how I might run encounters with the fae in other games.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-27T14:36:57-07:00","id":1491521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1491435-fae-immortality-and-reincarnation-through-their-mortal-hosts?p=1491490#post1491490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yet, the Changelings of CtD aren't about fairies of traditional folklore, they're about an amalgam creature unique to the setting more than any other splat. That's not a bug, it is how the game was intended to be, for better or worse. And the struggle to balance fae and mortal self is a key point to that.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is actually a big problem I have with the setting. I find actual fairy folklore and legends a lot more interesting than the original features of the game so I am actively trying to &quot;fix&quot; the game while keeping it as recognizable as I can. I don't expect full fidelity, that's impossible. There's a lot of contradictory lore out there, and you still need a final product that is somewhat gameable. But in the end, the setting of CtD does not show the fae in a way that is recognizable to me. It's clearly a derivative product that has departed from its source material. Blame my love for the Time-Life Books series The Enchanted World.<br /> <br /> It's not a problem that others do enjoy the setting as is. But I want something different in my own chronicles that is closer to the actual folklore. I do find many of the <i>concepts</i> of the game to be useful. Some are quite excellent in fact. I just interpret them differently to create a different final product.<br /> <br /> But your comments and explanation of how you run it at your table are very helpful to me, so thanks. Still interested in hearing how others approach it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-27T17:15:50-07:00","id":1491535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1491435-fae-immortality-and-reincarnation-through-their-mortal-hosts?p=1491519#post1491519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking of something similar to Glamour vs Banality, but couldn't figure out how to easily adjudicate that. I don't have C20 and can't see that chart. I do have 2e corebook and that has a Mists chart, but it only tracks Banality.</div> </div> </div> </div>No change, the chart only tracks Banality. It is almost unchanged, actually.<br /> <br /> What is different is that they added 2 charts about the effects of the Mists when entering and leaving the Dreaming, ando those do track Glamour - Banality, going from 10 to -10. But they're not that helpful and I personally find them too strict to be used. By their standards even upon entering the Dreaming itself a character with just 1 Glamour <b>over</b> Banality wouldn't ever have any recollection of past lives at all, from then it only devolves into degrees of incapable at operating in the Dreaming. With 2 Glamour over they need 1 entire month in the Dreaming to recover memory (and you only need 1 week to start accruing Nightmare, the basic marker of Bedlam). You need to be <b>Six Over</b> to have any meaningful recollection immediately and yet dismissing them as BS (although you overcome this in 3 turns).<br /> <br /> Banality and Glamour aren't opposites despite being conflicting forces. Glamour is a measure of power and magic, not connection to the Dreaming, while Banality <b>is</b> a measure of how much the Autumn World is cutting you from there (so to speak). For me the only thing those charts managed was to show that trying to unify the two to manage Mist effects doesn't really work well. I'd really recommend to stick to Banality on this and use the usual chart as basis. If you're decided to make Glamour a part of this, make your own rule, it will be better. Maybe the square root of your Glamour subtracts from Banality, to keep it from being too unpredictable? That would be just a -1 to -3 benefit, quite manageable. Half could work, but full Ban-Glam would be a mess.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1491435-fae-immortality-and-reincarnation-through-their-mortal-hosts?p=1491521#post1491521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is actually a big problem I have with the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm aware of that, it was just to make it clear that the distinction is of intent, not simply execution. Also to explain/contextualize what the setting does give. But by all means, I think your take is valid for your game and am also interested in what others can add to the conversation.<br /> <br /> For whatever it's worth, though, the whole idea of Changelings being affected by the Mists exists only to keep the intended amalgam soul theme. As the damage by the sun in Vampire it is extremely important to the <i>experience</i> of the game, but if this specific experience isn't your goal it has zero impact otherwise. Even in the game as is Changelings with full or almost full memories do not change things too seriously. You could just strike it off or even use another line's rules.<br /> <br /> My suggestion, if no one else gives feedback that may change this, would be to ditch CtD rules entirely <i>for this purpose</i> and use both the descriptions and the Background rules from <i>Demon the Fallen</i>. As far as identity goes, I think it works better for your intent and would be a neat version of the game without need to affect the rest of it.<br /> <br /> Other than that, <i>Changeling the Lost</i> is far more focused in the original legends and may interest you. If you go after it, I would recommend to you specifically the supplement <i>Equinox Road</i>. Rules aside, I think it will fit your concepts and needs more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}